        "explanation": "The V8 Engine (used in Chrome and Node.js) follows a specific pipeline to execute code:\n1. **Parser:** Parses raw JS source code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).\n2. **Ignition (Interpreter):** Converts the AST into unoptimized Bytecode and starts executing it immediately to make the page load fast.\n3. **Profiler:** Watches the executing code to see which functions are run repeatedly (called 'hot' functions).\n4. **TurboFan (JIT Compiler):** Takes those 'hot' functions and recompiles them into highly optimized Machine Code (Just-In-Time) for maximum speed. If assumptions change (like variable types), it 'Deoptimizes' back to bytecode.",
        "example": "This dual-tier system (Interpreter for fast startup, Compiler for peak performance) is why JS is so incredibly fast today.",
